INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES
(16278,91042,77039,'Suzanne Smith Sundburg','example@example.com',NULL,'127.213.72.64','Although your bill, Sen. VanValkenburg, sounds well-meaning, it could result in dramatic and unwanted/unintended negative consequences for local communities. \n\nDemand for water, sanitary sewer, electricity, public safety, schools, and even fire-safety services for residential development can be much greater than for existing commercial development. Bypassing local zoning and planning processes by automatically making construction of multifamily housing on all commercial or business-zoned land by right without also requiring adequate planning to support such development/redevelopment is a big mistake. Moreover, it short-circuits a locality\'s ability to ask developers for any contributions to support basic infrastructure improvements that an increased residential population will require.\n\nOver the past 20–30 years Arlington County has continually attempted to graft larger 21-century population density onto basic infrastructure (roads, parking, sanitary sewer, storm sewer, electrical grid, schools, even fire hydrants) designed and constructed largely in the 1940s and 1950s—when Arlington\'s population was smaller and less dense. Even with the site plan special exception process in place, the results of densification in Arlington have not been pretty for those of us living here.\n\n• For example, Arlington\'s fire hydrant system is antiquated: https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/why-are-arlingtons-fire-hydrants-the-colors-they-are/2013/02/23/2ad31ff2-7d02-11e2-9a75-dab0201670da_story.html_. It was NEVER designed to meet the demand of multifamily housing everywhere—particularly high-rise multifamily development. \n\nThe more kitchens you have on a site, the more likely you are to have fires. See Figure 7: Fire Incident Density in this report—POPULATION GROWTH, RISK ASSESSMENT, AND FIRE STATION LOCATION STUDY: https://arlingtonva.s3.dualstack.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/5/2015/04/Fire-Station-Locations-Risk-Assessement-FINAL-REPORT-12-14-12.pdf.\n\nLikewise, fighting multifamily fires tends to be more labor-intensive and time-consuming, leaving fire-and-rescue units less able to respond to other fires or emergency situations in a timely manner. Newer construction burns faster, leaving less time for occupants to evacuate safely: https://info.lifesafetyservices.com/modern-construction-materials-burn-faster. Whereas with older construction years ago people may have had 10–15 minutes to evacuate, today people may have only 2 or 3 minutes to evacuate when a fire occurs. Every minute counts now.\n\nMoreover, communities in VA may lack fire engines with ladders (which are expensive) that are capable of reaching residents in high-rise multifamily buildings (note that ladder trucks typically cannot reach above the 6th floor of a building). Here\'s a multifamily fire in New Jersey that took 15 hours for firefighters to put out: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EFPzS5ZBEyc\n\n• Water and sanitary sewer demand for a residential building typically exceeds the demand for water/sewer needed to operate a comparable-size office building. Commercial office tenants tend not to take showers, wash clothes and dishes, etc. So water and sewer infrastructure must be right-sized in order to meet the future demand on the existing systems. \n\nIn Arlington, lateral lines in less densely populated areas simply weren\'t designed (in the 1940s/1950s) to handle the water/sewer needs for a much larger population. Even in very dense parts of the county, like Rosslyn (sanitary sewer line built in the 1930s) and Ft. Myer (water line), for example, the water/sewer pipes are antiques and subject to catastrophic failure as demand on the system has increased: https://www.arlnow.com/2022/03/18/county-board-to-consider-1-1-million-water-main-project-near-rosslyn/ and https://arlington.granicus.com/MetaViewer.php?view_id=2&event_id=1854&meta_id=221069. \n\nCapacity is only one aspect, age/condition is another. In Arlington, both problems exist. If you type in \"water main break\" into the search box on local media outlet ARLnow.com\'s website, you will find scores of water main break notices, as our water lines in many cases are well beyond their useful lives: https://www.google.com/search?q=water+main+break+site%3Awww.arlnow.com.\n\n• Arlington\'s electrical grid is in many areas stretched to capacity. That\'s true especially in the densely redeveloped Rosslyn-Ballston (R-B) and Rt. 1/National Landing/Crystal/Pentagon City corridors. Here are images and video of a 2016 electrical vault explosion and fire in Clarendon due to overloading conditions: https://www.arlnow.com/2016/06/21/developing-transformer-fire-at-market-common-clarendon/.\n\nHere\'s another electrical/utility explosion and fire in 2024 in Crystal City: https://www.arlnow.com/2024/07/23/developing-underground-utility-fire-reported-after-sound-of-explosion-in-crystal-city/. Power outages are common and sometimes prolonged in Arlington County, as a search for \"power outage\" on ARLnow.com illustrates: https://www.google.com/search?q=power+outage%3Awww.arlnow.com&sca_esv=fb57592daf8c32c6&biw=1672&bih=831&ei=8GmFZ9TRAavS5NoPqLXJiAk&ved=0ahUKEwjUievdt_OKAxUrKVkFHahaEpE4WhDh1QMIEA&uact=5&oq=power+outage%3Awww.arlnow.com&gs_lp=Egxnd3Mtd2l6LXNlcnAaAhgCIhtwb3dlciBvdXRhZ2U6d3d3LmFybG5vdy5jb21ImBlQrQVYhxdwAXgAkAEAmAF1oAHzBaoBBDExLjG4AQPIAQD4AQGYAgGgAn7CAg4QABiiBBj4BRiJBRiLA8ICCBAAGIsDGO8FmAMAiAYBkgcDMC4xoAfzCA&sclient=gws-wiz-serp.\n\nResidential multifamily buildings, depending on density, can increase demand on an already overtaxed grid: https://www.epa.gov/energy/electricity-customers. The taller the building, the greater the demand for power grows: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2017/jun/high-rise-buildings-much-more-energy-intensive-low-rise. \n\nAnd because so much of Arlington is heavily developed with a high (and growing) percentage of heat-trapping, water-shedding impervious surfaces and structures, the urban heat island effect magnifies high summer temperatures and thus increases demand and stress on the electrical grid. \n\nAll that being said, there are grid constraints throughout Arlington (even in parts of the county with lower density). Listen to commissioner Claire Noakes of the Joint Facilities Advisory Commission (JFAC) in this 11-16-2022 meeting video, beginning at the 36-minute mark on the time counter: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x8cLgF9a2zA. In a subsequent 2024 conversation with another resident, she explained that Dominion Energy had cautioned Arlington County that it must take into account transmission and distribution infrastructure when planning for new facilities and construction to ensure that the grid could provide sufficient power.\n\nThese are just 3 examples that demonstrate how problematic the by-right conversion of commercial buildings or properties from office (or retail or whatever) to multifamily may be without requiring—or even allowing—localities to properly plan in advance for increased density and service/infrastructure demands that may arise from such development/redevelopment.\n\nHousing may be needed in certain locations, but there are reality-driven constraints that limit how much housing and how many people a community can support. \n\nIn addition to infrastructure concerns, it is generally acknowledged that residential development does NOT pay for itself in terms of net revenue received by the locality from residents: https://richmond.com/opinion/editorial/opinion-blaming-localities-for-the-housing-crisis-is-misguided/article_0cf6eff1-e9b9-59aa-a1cd-59e4e2704cd4.html. \n\nThat is certainly true here in Arlington, where, according to an analysis of GMU economist Dr. Stephen Fuller, Arlington spends, on average, $850 more per resident to provide services and infrastructure than it collects from residents in revenue: (see Table 6) https://sfullerinstitute.gmu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2018/11/SFI_-Economic_-Fiscal_Impacts_of_Amazon-HQ2_110818.pdf.\n\nI urge you to reconsider your legislation\'s by-right provision. At a minimum, I ask you to add firm, sensible guardrails that require any development or redevelopment to be contingent upon responsible planning to include basic infrastructure as well as costs to the community so that new and existing residents are not plagued by inadequate basic services, unsafe conditions, and/or tax bills that grow unsustainably to absorb these costs. \n\nI can see why this legislation is popular with commercial property owners and developers, as it enables them to privatize their profits and to socialize all of the costs by leaving residents and local governments holding the bag. Is this what you truly intended?','comment','published','y','2025-01-13 20:24:59','2025-01-13 20:24:59');
INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES
(16254,91012,76995,'Hans','example@example.com',NULL,'127.242.184.215','Please vote against SB 805, which would carry Virginia even further away from compliance with federal child-support regulations, by imposing excessive child support obligations on thousands of parents that they will be unable to pay, and leave them unable to meet their basic needs. SB 805\'s schedule of basic child support obligations violates 45 CFR § 302.56(c)(1).\n\n45 CFR § 302.56(c)(1) requires state child-support guidelines to take into account the \"ability to pay\" and \"the basic subsistence needs of the noncustodial parent.\"\n\nTo comply with that regulation, adopted in 2016, Virginia\'s neighbors, such as North Carolina, Maryland, and Tennessee, do not impose significant child support obligations on parents making less than $1300 per month, because parents that poor cannot afford to pay much child support while housing and feeding themselves.  (In Maryland, the basic child support amount is $50-$60 per month for parents making $1200 or less per month, and $109-$124 per month for parents making $1300 per month. See Md. Code 12-204.In North Carolina, the basic child support obligation is only $50 per month for parents making $1300 per month. See North Carolina Schedule of Basic Support Obligations, effective January 1, 2023). In Tennessee, the child-support \"guidelines include a SSR [self-support reserve] that ensures obligors have sufficient income to maintain a minimum standard of living based on 90% of the 2020 federal poverty level for one person ($957 gross income per month).\" See page 11, Rules of the Tennessee Department of Human Services, Child Support Services Division, Chapter 1240-02-04, Child Support Guideline.)\n\nBut under this child-support bill (SB 805), the basic child support obligation is an unaffordable $264 to $665 per month for households making $1300 per month (depending on the number of kids they have). For parents making $550 per month, basic child support is between $114 and $287 per month, which is well beyond their ability to pay. \n\nSo under the bill, even parents way below the poverty level have to pay 20% to 52% of their income in basic child support -- leaving them with too little money to pay rent! Even the poorest parents unable to pay child support are ordered to pay a big fraction of their income in child support under the bill.\n\nSB 805 not only continues to impose child support obligations on parents making as little as $350 per month (most of whom simply cannot pay such obligations), it increases child support obligations for households with incomes as little as $550 per month. At some higher income ranges, the increases in child support are over 30%, and child support obligors have to pay tens of thousands of dollars per year in child support.\n \nIt is impractical for the bill to increase child support obligations for parents making very low incomes such as $550 per month or even $2,000 per month, which is still below the income made by many minimum wage workers in Virginia (the state hourly minimum wage is $12.41).\n\nAnd this is just part of the child-support many parents owe. SB 805 increases the single most important component of child-support obligations, the schedule of basic child support obligations contained in Va. Code § 20-108.2(B)..\n\nBut that is just the beginning of what many parents owe in child support. Additional amounts of child support are also imposed on noncustodial parents under other existing provisions of Virginia law, such as for \"health care coverage\", see Va. Code § 20-108.2(E), and for \"child care\" costs, see Va. Code § 20-108.2(F), which are awarded on top of the schedule of basic child support obligations. For low-income parents, those amounts can be bigger than the amount of basic child support, resulting in an overall child-support obligation that consumes most of their income (even before taxes). For example, a father was ordered to pay $673 in monthly child support on his monthly income of just $1300, back when Virginia\'s child support obligations were lower than they are today, see the Virginia Court of Appeals decision in Herring v. Herring, 532 S.E.2d 923, 925 (2000).\n\nSo even if parents could pay the amounts contained in SB 805\'s schedule of basic child support obligations (many parents could not), they still might not be able to pay their overall child support obligation.Even the portions of the child support schedule (in SB 805) that look reasonable on first glance may not be, because they are just part of the child support obligations imposed on many parents, which include things like additional child care expenses.\n\nI am not a noncustodial parent, but I and my wife have been parents for nearly 18 years, and based on my experience as a parent, it costs less to raise a kid than the Virginia child-support guidelines typically award in child support for middle class parents like us. Thus, the guidelines are more than adequate and do not need to be increased further.\n\nExcessive child support levels can backfire by reducing the earning capacity of noncustodial parents and leaving them even less able to pay taxes and child support in the future. In 2019, The Baltimore Sun quoted a former federal head of child support enforcement, Vicki Turetsky, noting that \"Child support orders set beyond the ability of noncustodial parents to comply push them out of low-wage jobs, drown them in debt, hound them into the underground economy, and chase them out of their children’s lives.\"\n\nAs the Baltimore Sun noted, when noncustodial parents \"fall behind on payments, they can lose their driver’s licenses and professional licenses...The enforcement tactics can make it hard for them to catch back up on payments...Evidence shows fathers who get into deep debt are less engaged with their kids, contributing to greater rates of depression, alcohol use, poor health and progressively worse behavior by the children.\"\n\nChild support obligations are often set too high to begin with. When California commissioned the Urban Institute to investigate why parents were often behind on their child support, it reported that the number one reason for arrearages was that “orders are set too high relative to ability to pay.”\n\nI was the intake attorney for a nonprofit law firm for 6 years. We didn\'t handle child-support or family law cases, but despite that, I received innumerable letters and emails from people ordered to pay more than they could possibly afford in child support. It was obvious they were drowning in debt, and unlikely to ever pay what they owed in full. SB805 would make that problem worse.','comment','published','n','2025-01-04 01:39:03','2025-01-04 01:39:03'),
(16255,91012,76995,'Hans','example@example.com',NULL,'127.227.49.158','Existing child-support obligations are already excessive for large numbers of low-income noncustodial parents, due to the child-support guidelines\' failure to take into account the public benefits that go to custodial parents and most of the tax benefits that go to custodial parents. Child support obligations are often excessive because Virginia\'s child support guidelines don\'t take into account tax benefits and other public benefits custodial parents get on account of their kids (such as the $2,000 per child tax credit, subsidized housing, TANF, etc.), in determining the amount of child support that the custodial parent needs and the non-custodial parent owes. That results in the non-custodial parent paying household expenses of the custodial parent that are already paid for by the tax code and other sources -- expenses low-income noncustodial parents can\'t afford to pay while also paying for their own housing, food & clothing. For example, Virginia Code Section 20-108.2(C) says, \"\'Gross income\' shall not include: 1. Benefits from public assistance and social services programs as defined in § 63.2-100; 2. Federal supplemental security income benefits...\" Virginia law also defines as income to the noncustodial parent things that aren\'t income under the federal tax code.... \"custodial parents will get the child tax credits (and other credits).\" But these tax credits aren\'t treated as income to the custodial parent in calculating the child support needed to be paid to the custodial parent, even though they are effectively income to the custodial parent.\"\n\nThe $2,000 child tax credit, earned-income tax credits, and other public benefits don\'t reduce the noncustodial parent\'s child-support obligation. In Virginia, unlike most states, judges apparently have no authority to order the custodial parent to waive the child tax exemption or credit so that it may be claimed by the non-custodial parent, even if the non-custodial parent provides most of the financial support for the child. See Floyd v. Floyd, 436 S.E.2d 451, 463 (Va. 1997); Pearlene Anklesaria, Child-Related Tax Breaks for Divorced Parents, 22 J. Am. Acad. of Matrim. L. 425, 426-27 (2009) (contrasting Virginia law with the law of most states). The custodial parent is also more able than the non-custodial parent to claim the Earned Income Tax Credit. Those tax credits and other public benefits don\'t reduce the non-custodial parent\'s child support obligation. See, e.g., Va. Code § 20-108.2(C) (defining \"gross income\" to exclude \"Benefits from public assistance and social services programs\").\n\nSo SB 805 would make matters worse by increasing already-excessive child-support obligations of many low-income parents.','comment','published','n','2025-01-05 16:48:30','2025-01-05 16:48:30');
INSERT INTO `comments` VALUES
(16272,91026,78195,'Bill Peabody','example@example.com',NULL,'127.191.70.0','Home schooled generally  outperform public school kids. This bill is a no.','comment','published','n','2025-01-12 19:55:28','2025-01-12 19:55:28'),
(16288,91055,78195,'Suzi Croes','example@example.com','https://vicfa.org','127.47.90.176','Senator Pekarsky, assumes that religious homeschoolers need monitoring to make sure that they are up to \"her\" standards of learning  as a public servant in the educational system.  That will never do.  It is like saying that apples and oranges are the same because they are both fruit.  They are-but very different.  She should respect the dedication it takes to homeschool and the individual right to raise our children with religious convictions of our choosing.','comment','published','n','2025-01-16 05:51:30','2025-01-16 05:51:30');
